00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.630
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We're
here with Tim, CTO

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.160
from Techolution,
who'll be sharing

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.660
with us a bit more
about Techolution

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.400
and how they implement Anthos.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.560
But first, Tim, can
you tell us a bit more

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.960
about what Techolution
does and what it means

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.280
to be a Google Cloud partner?

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:17.640
TIM DELESIO: Sure.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.143
Well first of all, thanks Mark
for having me on the show.

00:00:20.143 --> 00:00:21.060
It's great to be here.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.750
Techolution helps companies come
in, modernize their application

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.670
stack, and really come
up with a strategy

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:27.803
on how to get to the cloud.

00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:29.970
And then we'll go in, set
up their cloud environment

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.997
for them, make sure it's
secure, and ready to be

00:00:32.997 --> 00:00:34.080
deploying applications to.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.060
And then we'll even run
manage services at the end,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:36.910
if they're interested.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:37.930
I work with a lot of companies.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.810
And they really, really
want to modernize the place.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.160
And they want to run
containers and they

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.450
want to run in their
data center or maybe

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.740
multiple cloud providers.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.195
And the options that are out
there are really limited.

00:00:46.195 --> 00:00:47.820
And they all have
some sort of drawback

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.130
and some sort of things
that really stop you

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.250
from working with it.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.380
Whereas Anthos,
it really doesn't.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.985
I mean, there's a lot of
amazing things with it.

00:00:53.985 --> 00:00:55.650
It's based on open
source technology.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.480
It frees you from
the vendor lock

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.220
and you can deploy
it to many clouds.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.170
It has the power
of GKE, so it helps

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.640
you run the cloud for you.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.130
And it really is-- it's
not that expensive.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.797
So there's just a lot
of benefits to it.

00:01:07.797 --> 00:01:09.213
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That makes sense

00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:10.540
for how Anthos can help people.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.270
But how does it actually
do those individual things?

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.520
TIM DELESIO: Anthos is
actually a collection of tools.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.610
I should say, a collection
of mostly open source tools,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.443
which even brings a lot
of more power to it.

00:01:19.443 --> 00:01:21.750
So first you have GKE on
Prem, which brings you

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.152
the power of GKE and all
those benefits, like I said,

00:01:25.152 --> 00:01:26.610
that Google brings
forward for you.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.680
Manages the clusters for you.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.420
It allows you that scalability.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.310
And all the amazing things
that come along with it.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.760
Second, you got the Anthos
configuration manager, which

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.530
is a great piece
of technology that

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.260
allows you to treat your
configurations as code so you

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.358
can check them into the
repository, make changes.

00:01:42.358 --> 00:01:44.400
And when you want to change
or update the system,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.990
it'll automatically
pull it down.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.970
The next is the
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.220
which is a product
based on ISTIO, which

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.990
allows you to kind
of get some insight

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.610
into your inter-process
communication

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:56.580
between your containers and
get some insight into actually

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.360
what's going on.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.520
And next on the list,
you have Cloud Run,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.310
which is a server
list technology that

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.050
allows you to
bring the functions

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.898
and the service list benefits
you get to your data center

00:02:06.898 --> 00:02:08.190
or to any other cloud provider.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.090
And then finally, you
have the Marketplace.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.960
So if you need to use third
party tools like MongoDB,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.360
or Redis or Elasticsearch
or any of the things

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.310
that are actually
in the marketplace,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.440
you can bring those to wherever
you're actually running Anthos.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So it makes sense

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.380
these different
pieces kind of play

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.210
together to create a
really great solution.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:24.610
TIM DELESIO: Yes, yes.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.783
MARK MIRCHANDANI: For a
wide variety of use cases.

00:02:26.783 --> 00:02:28.950
Can you talk a bit more
about how you've implemented

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.542
Anthos in a specific use case?

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:32.250
And like, what that
brought to the table?

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.250
TIM DELESIO: Absolutely.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.520
Anthos is still
relatively new technology.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.140
You know, it just
came out last April.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.140
So people are still learning
to get their hands on it

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.520
and figure out how it works.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.600
So we've been working
with a lot of customers.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.230
And when they embark on
this journey by themselves,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.540
you know, and they don't
properly plan and strategize

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.120
how they're going to do this,
they kind of run into problems.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.520
We've saved many customers
coming in and helping

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:54.600
them strategize.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.010
So we're able to come
in, set up a plan,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.060
use some of our automation
and our IP that we've created

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.340
and help them get to
Anthos in 30 days or less.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.840
Another good scenario
that we worked with,

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.360
which is very,
very specific one,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.770
is we're working with a client.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.030
And the IT operations
guy probably

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.030
had a little too much
power than he should have.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.718
And he was working
with the environment

00:03:13.718 --> 00:03:15.510
and he was in a qual
environment-- at least

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.020
he thought he was in
a qual environment.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.020
But he was actually in a
production environment.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.300
And he accidentally
deleted the namespace.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.420
And you can't imagine what the
repercussions of that were.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.100
So with Anthos,
if he was running

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.080
Anthos and those
Configuration Manager,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.550
he wouldn't have
had that problem.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.100
Because if you
delete the namespace,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.830
it will automatically go back
to the single source of truth,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.560
a.k.a., the repository,
pull the latest version

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.020
of whatever it was, and
rebuild the cluster for you.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.390
So actually, after we
deployed Anthos for him,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.970
we actually showed him this
and had him actually do it.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.120
And he was amazed at
how quick it actually

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.870
rebuilt the cluster
right in front of him.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:49.530
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I'm sure that's

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.958
a situation that unfortunately,
a lot of us can relate to.

00:03:51.958 --> 00:03:53.250
TIM DELESIO: Unfortunately yes.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.833
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
But it kind of gives

00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:57.090
a good example of where
the power of Anthos

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.660
and implementing it comes from.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.077
So for people who are interested
in maybe taking on Anthos

00:04:01.077 --> 00:04:02.743
or learning more about
it, what are Some

00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:04.110
of the big takeaways for them?

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:04.500
TIM DELESIO: Sure.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.210
The biggest thing that I
think that you kind of don't

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.752
necessarily think about is it
really brings the cloud to you,

00:04:08.752 --> 00:04:10.300
allows you to
modernize in place.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.690
So if you want to run
containers and micro services,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.160
and you want to run
that in your data center

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.160
or really any cloud
provider that you choose,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.760
it brings that
capabilities for you.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.829
And it brings all the power of
GKE and the management of that.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:22.410
It's also great
for companies that

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.170
have problems with either
regulations, whether they

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.087
be data regulations or
government regulations,

00:04:27.087 --> 00:04:29.378
where they've got to keep
all of the data in one place.

00:04:29.378 --> 00:04:31.140
You can bring the
cloud to them where you

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.340
don't have to get the data out.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.798
You know, some of
the big takeaways

00:04:33.798 --> 00:04:36.810
that I've learned from actually
setting up and installing

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.593
Anthos is make sure that you
have full logging enabled.

00:04:39.593 --> 00:04:41.010
I mean, you need
to be able to see

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.080
every log item that goes in,
whether it's info or to bug.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.250
The more logging you have,
the better you'll be.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.000
And second, you know,
when you're actually

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.410
going in and
setting this up, you

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.258
need to be really good
at recreating this.

00:04:51.258 --> 00:04:52.800
You need to be able
to wipe it clean,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.800
start fresh, and just keep
going and plugging away.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.060
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's always
great to hear these customer

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:57.400
stories.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.317
So thank you so much for
coming in and telling

00:04:59.317 --> 00:05:01.418
us a bit more about what
makes Anthos so powerful.

00:05:01.418 --> 00:05:02.460
TIM DELESIO: Thanks Mark.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.370
Thanks for having me.

